WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.110
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
Kubernetes is helping

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:02.520
develop our own
operations teams,

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:04.760
deploying more complex
applications than ever.

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:07.110
When rolling out a new release
or making other changes

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:08.700
to our production
services, we need

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.040
to make sure we're
prepared for any issues.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.710
Stackdriver is Google's fully
featured operations management

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:15.880
[? feed ?] for
Cloud-powered applications.

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:18.505
Let's take a look at how it can
help you keep your applications

00:00:18.505 --> 00:00:19.480
and services healthy.

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:21.241
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:00:21.241 --> 00:00:25.595
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:25.595 --> 00:00:27.470
As you might have heard,
the Stackdriver team

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:29.053
has been working
hard to support using

00:00:29.053 --> 00:00:30.470
Stackdriver with Kubernetes.

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:34.460
It's still in beta, but I'm here
today to give you a sneak peek.

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:37.040
The first and obvious thing,
we need a Kubernetes cluster

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:37.800
to monitor.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:38.787
Let's create one.

00:00:38.787 --> 00:00:40.370
And there is no
better or easier place

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:43.970
to do that than Google
Kubernetes Engine, or GKE.

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:46.580
Now, the beta feature is only
available for clusters running

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:49.020
Kubernetes version
1.10.6 or later,

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:50.660
so let's create one of those.

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:52.201
Here's the gcloud
command we're going

00:00:52.201 --> 00:00:53.700
to use to create our cluster.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.370
Note the enables
Stackdriver Kubernetes

00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:57.800
flag that opts us into the
beta monitoring and logging

00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:58.770
features.

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:01.130
We also need the
cluster-version=latest flag

00:01:01.130 --> 00:01:02.910
to make sure they run
a supported version,

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:05.090
at least 1.10.6.

00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:06.714
Once the cluster
is up and running,

00:01:06.714 --> 00:01:08.630
we'll want to see exactly
what's running that.

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:11.660
And what better way to do
that than with kubectl.

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:14.210
We have the monitoring and
logging agents, the metadata

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:16.800
agent, and heaps are all
running in a Stackdriver agents

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:17.930
namespace.

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:19.410
These will collect
all the details

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:21.997
we need from our nodes
running in the cluster.

00:01:21.997 --> 00:01:24.330
Now that we have a cluster
with monitoring set up on it,

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:26.210
let's take a look at the data.

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:28.340
When we go to Stackdriver
by clicking on Monitoring

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:30.110
from the Products
and Services menu

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:31.750
and look at the
Resources menu, we

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:34.040
can see a new entry
called Kubernetes beta.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:36.630
This is the new
Kubernetes monitoring UI.

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:37.414
Let's take a look.

00:01:37.414 --> 00:01:39.080
The first thing you'll
notice is that we

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:41.400
have three different ways
of looking at our cluster.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:43.899
The infrastructure view may be
more useful for understanding

00:01:43.899 --> 00:01:45.860
the physical layout of
your cluster, nodes,

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:47.622
and what's running where.

00:01:47.622 --> 00:01:49.080
The workloads view
is more relevant

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:51.038
if you care about what's
running in the cluster

00:01:51.038 --> 00:01:53.270
and namespaces more so
than about the nodes.

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:55.040
This may be relevant
for folks responsible

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:56.880
for the applications
and services,

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:58.620
such as the DevOps teams.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:00.290
And finally, the
services view can

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:02.290
help you understand how
workloads in the cluster

00:02:02.290 --> 00:02:05.450
are exposed as services, load
balancers, ingress controllers,

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:06.172
and the like.

00:02:06.172 --> 00:02:07.880
And this might be most
relevant if you're

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:09.839
attempting to troubleshoot
an issue, attempting

00:02:09.839 --> 00:02:12.171
to communicate with the
services running on the cluster,

00:02:12.171 --> 00:02:14.150
and don't necessarily
need to know about what's

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:16.325
behind the services themselves.

00:02:16.325 --> 00:02:18.950
Note that every view allows you
to click on any of the elements

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:20.610
to get more details about them.

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:22.610
You can see things like
open incidents affecting

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:24.720
that particular
component, other events,

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:27.260
such as container restarts,
and performance and utilization

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:28.020
metrics.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:31.010
Even logs are integrated into
this view for easy access.

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:33.710
Now that our cluster is up
and monitoring is in place,

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:36.230
we can use Stackdriver to ensure
that our application stays

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:36.809
healthy.

00:02:36.809 --> 00:02:38.600
Come back next time
when we use Stackdriver

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:41.060
to identify, triage,
and diagnose problems

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:42.530
on our application.

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:43.850
That's it for this episode.

00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:45.200
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:46.490
Stay healthy out there.

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:49.840
[MUSIC PLAYING]

